01:00:51 [f75e] Concept level (ages 8): School-age conceptual reasoning involving explicit category relations, cross-classification, hierarchical reasoning, causal explanation, food-origin/processing reasoning, problem-solution reasoning, and structured narrative understanding.
01:00:51 [f75e] Wording: Questions can be longer and can ask for explanation, comparison, or reasoning, but should remain concrete and contextually grounded.
01:00:51 [f75e] Question length: 8-14 words when expressive language level supports it.
01:00:51 [f75e] Avoid: fully abstract reasoning without concrete context; adult-level nutrition or biology mechanisms; technical food production; highly ambiguous category exceptions; complex social inference without story support
01:00:51 [f75e] Target these concepts (vary across the question set):
01:00:51 [f75e] - familiar event script: Ask about common actions, objects, people, or places in familiar events. e.g. Do books go in backpacks?
01:00:51 [f75e] - event sequence and planning: Ask whether an action happens before, after, or to prepare for a familiar event. e.g. Do we pack before school?
01:00:51 [f75e] - taxonomic thematic relation: Ask how two items are related by category or by everyday use/event context. e.g. How are apples and bananas alike?
01:00:51 [f75e] - problem solution reasoning: Ask what someone should do when a familiar problem occurs. e.g. What can you do if a pencil breaks?
01:00:51 [f75e] - causal explanation: Ask why something happens or why an object/action is useful. e.g. Why do we use umbrellas?
01:00:51 [f75e] - prediction inference: Ask the child to make a simple prediction or inference from a familiar context. e.g. What might happen when clouds get dark?
01:00:51 [f75e] - within food category: Ask whether a food belongs to a specific food category. e.g. Is an apple a fruit?
01:00:51 [f75e] - cross classification: Ask whether one item can be classified in multiple valid ways. e.g. Is an apple both fruit and food?
01:00:51 [f75e] Familiar items per topic: School: backpack, book, pencil, teacher, classroom, paper | Home: bed, cup, table, chair, toothbrush, clothes | Food: bread, milk, cheese, eggs, flour, pasta | Nature: tree, flower, bird, fish, rain, sun
